\section{Overview}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Objectives and overview}

\mode<article>{
  This section introduces the idea of nesting.
  At this point, the designs and data start to become a lot more complex, but otherwise the ideas and principles directly follow what we have already discussed this semester.
  This material really represents the culmination of each of the topics we have covered thus far: randomization, CRDs, Blocking, Random and Mixed effects models, etc.
  
  As you study this material, be patient with yourself. Our textbook author readily acknowledges that this material can be difficult to comprehend the first time you see it; the best way to understand this material is practice!
  
  In this section, we will start to use data coming from measurement units, rather than needing to aggregate these data by experimental unit.
  This is possible with appropriate nesting and use of random effects.
}

  \begin{itemize}
    \item This material is based Chapter~12 on the textbook ``A First Course in Design and Analysis of Experiments" \citep{oehlert2000}.
    \item Chapter outcomes include: 
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Students will be able to define both crossed and nested effects, and articulate the differences.
      \item Students will be able to fit mixed effects models to data with nested or crossed designs.
      \item Students will be able understand Hasse diagrams, and be able to build the basic ``skeleton" of these diagrams.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{Nesting vs. Crossing}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Nesting Versus Crossing}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Up to this point, our multi-factor designs have utilized \alert{crossed factors}.
    
    \begin{block}{Definition: Crossed Factors}
      Factors $A$ and $B$ are said to be \alert{crossed} if treatments are formed by all possible combinations of the levels of the factors.
      That is, the same levels of Factor A are applied across all levels of Factor B.
    \end{block}
    
    \begin{exampleblock}{Example: Replicated Latin Squares (LS)}
      In a replicated LS design, if we reuse one of the block factors (e.g., row blocks) across the squares, then the \textbf{Row} and \textbf{Square} factors are \alert{crossed}, because each row sees each block.
    \end{exampleblock}
    
    \begin{block}{Definition: Nested Factors}
      Factor B is \textit{nested} in Factor A if there is a completely different, unique set of levels of B for every level of A.
      That is, some levels of $B$ only exist \emph{within} certain levels of $A$.
    \end{block}
 
    \begin{exampleblock}{Example: Replicated Latin Squares (LS)}
      In a replicated LS design, if we \emph{do not} reuse any blocking factors, (i.e., unique row and column blocks for each square), then the \textbf{Row} and \textbf{Column} factors are said to be \alert{nested} in \textbf{square}.
    \end{exampleblock}

  \end{itemize}

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Fixed vs Random effects}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Nested factors can be random or fixed.
    \item A common situation is a random effect, resulting from some kind of subsampling.
    
    \begin{exampleblock}{Random nested effect}
    Within brand, we randomly sampled jars. It makes sense for Brand to be fixed, whereas we are not interested in the specific jars we picked, rather we want to use them as a representative of the brand that it is nested in. Thus, $A$ (brand) should be fixed, and the nested factor $B$ should be random.
    \end{exampleblock}
    
    \framebreak
    
    \item Nested factors can also be fixed. 
    
       \begin{exampleblock}{Fixed nested effect}
    Consider a company with work crews. Each crew consists of four members.
    
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Members are nested in crews
      \item We get the same four crew members whenever we look at a given crew.
    \end{itemize}
    
    In this case, crew members would naturally be a fixed effect. 
    
    \end{exampleblock} 
    
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Example: Testing vitamin A content}

\begin{block}{Vitamin A study}
    Suppose you are concerned about differences in Vitamin A content within jars of baby food carrots. Our designed study: 
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Go to the grocery store, randomly select 4 jars from each of the 3 brands of baby food that are sold in our region.
      \item Take two samples from each jar and measure the vitamin A in every sample.
      \item In total, there are 24 responses (2 per jar, 4 jars per brand, 3 brands).
    \end{itemize}
\end{block}

\framebreak

\begin{itemize}
  \item In the study design above, there are 3 sources of variation:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Variation between brands.
    \item Variation between jars for each brand.
    \item Variation between samples for every jar.
  \end{itemize}
  \item It does \emph{not} make sense to consider the main-effect of jar, OR the brand-by-jar interaction.
  \mode<article>{For instance, Jar one for brand A has absolutely nothing to do with jar one for brand B; they might both have lots of vitamin A by chance, but it would just be chance. They are not linked, so there should be no jar main effect across the brands.
  
  Notably, if the main effect of Jar does not make sense, then neither does a jar by brand interaction, because the two-factor interaction can be interpreted as how the main effect of Jar must be altered at each level of brand.}
    % \item \textbf{Example (Baby Carrots):} We want to test Vitamin A consistency across 3 brands of baby food. We buy 4 jars of each brand, and take 2 samples from each jar.
    % \begin{itemize}
    %   \item Jar 1 of Brand A has absolutely nothing to do with Jar 1 of Brand B. 
    %   \item Jars are \textbf{nested} within Brand. They do not cross.
    % \end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak
  
    \item Let $A$ be the brand factor, $B$ the jar factor. A nested model for this situation may be:
    $$
    y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_{j(i)} + \epsilon_{k(ij)}.
    $$
    \begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha_i$: Fixed effect of Brand $i$.
    \item $\beta_{j(i)}$: Random effect of Jar $j$ \textit{nested within} Brand $i$.
    \item $\epsilon_{k(ij)}$: Random error of sample $k$ nested in Jar $j$ and Brand $i$.
    \end{itemize}
    We have the standard model assumptions of normality and independence of random terms.
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{Nesting in R}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks,fragile]{The Nesting Coding in R}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item How the data are encoded dictates your \texttt{lme4} syntax.
    \item \textbf{Implicit IDs:} If you number Jars $1, 2, 3, 4$ for Brand A, and $1, 2, 3, 4$ for Brand B, fitting \texttt{(1 | Jar)} is \alert{wrong}. The software assumes Jar 1 is crossed between brands.
    \item \textbf{Explicit IDs:} If you number Jars uniquely ($1 \dots 8$), \texttt{(1 | Jar)} works perfectly.
  \end{itemize}

<<carrots-example, eval=FALSE>>=
library(lme4)
library(lmerTest)

# Assume 'carrots' data is loaded (Oehlert, Ch 12)
# Brand is Fixed. Jar is Random.

# SCENARIO 1: Implicit IDs (Jars 1-4 repeated in each Brand)
# You MUST use the interaction operator ':' to nest Jar in Brand
mod_implicit <- lmer(VitA ~ Brand + (1 | Brand:Jar), data = carrots)

# SCENARIO 2: Explicit IDs (Jars uniquely numbered 1-8)
# lme4 automatically recognizes the nesting structure 
mod_explicit <- lmer(VitA ~ Brand + (1 | Jar), data = carrots)
@

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{R formula ``shortcuts"}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item There are R formula shortcuts. For instance, we have used already:
    \begin{center}
      \texttt{A*B} $\implies$ \texttt{A + B + A:B}
    \end{center}
    \item Similarly, there is a common nesting shortcut:
    \begin{center}
      \texttt{A/B} $\implies$ \texttt{A + A:B}
    \end{center}
    \item \textbf{We must be careful with fixed vs random effects!} shortcuts will give you either fixed, or random, but not both:
    \begin{center}
      \texttt{(1 | A/B)} $\implies$ \texttt{(1 | A) + (1 | A:B)}
    \end{center}
    This may not be what you want if $A$ is fixed and $B$ is a nested, random effect! 
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Why do we use Nesting?}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item We don't just ``choose" to use a nested model. We use it because the experimental logistics or physical constraints dictate it.
  \end{itemize}
    
    \begin{exampleblock}{Example: subsampling}
      A common example is \textbf{subsampling:} Taking smaller units from a larger selected ``bundle" (Carrot example: Brands $\rightarrow$ Jars $\rightarrow$ samples).
    \end{exampleblock}
    
    \begin{exampleblock}{Example: Logistical constraints}
      Sometimes nested designs are necessary due to logistical constraints:
      
      Suppose there are 3 specialized clinics in the country. We can't realistically send the same patient to all 3 clinics to ``cross" patients with clinics. Patients are nested in clinics.
    \end{exampleblock}

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Nesting vs Averaging MUs}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Distinctions between experimental units and measurements units can sometimes be explained by nested factors.
  \end{itemize}
  \begin{block}{Testing Feed on Pig Growth}
    Suppose we have multiple pens of pigs, and $g$ different feeds we would like to test.
    We randomly select pens to apply different feed, and observe the growth of individual pigs.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item The EUs are pig pens, the MUs are pigs.
      \item Previously: To test differences in treatment, we aggregated results over the measurement unit. 
      \item Blindly treating the pigs as EUs would lead to ``pseudo-replication", because randomization was not done to the MUs, which is why we aggregated.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  
  \framebreak
  
  \begin{itemize}
    \item This same design that we discussed really has a \alert{nested} structure.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \begin{exampleblock}{Nested Design: pig pens}
    In the same design, there are 3 sources of variation: Treatment effect $(A)$, pen effect $(B)$, and individual pig effect (error).
    
    Rather than aggregating MUs, we could instead use a nested model: pen ($B$) is nested in feed type ($A$) because Pen 1 only gets one level of the treatment.
  \end{exampleblock}
  
  \framebreak
  
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Which is the correct approach? \textbf{They Both are}!
    \item If the design is \alert{balanced}, the $F$ statistic and $P$ value for the main treatment effect (feed, factor $A$) \alert{will be the same} regardless of the approach we pick.
    \item This new approach (nested instead of averaging) does not change the treatment effect estimates, but now allows us to compare / estimate pen-to-pen variance $\sigma^2_{pen}$ against the pig-to-pig variance $\sigma^2$.
    \mode<article>{
    \item This information can be useful for designing future studies; for instance, if $\sigma^2$ is much larger than $\sigma^2_{pen}$, then future studies on the treatment effect should prioritize having more pigs-per-pen. If $\sigma^2_{pen}$ is much larger than $\sigma^2$, we should have fewer pigs-per-pen and prioritize more pens.
    }
  \end{itemize}
  
\end{frame}

\section{Crossed and Nested Factors: Examples}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks,fragile]{Example: Cheese tasting (Ex 12.2)}

\mode<article>{
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Cheese manufacturers want to understand how urban and rural consumers rate different types of cheeses. The study design is given below.
  \end{itemize}
}
  
  \begin{block}{Cheese tasting experiment}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item 4 different types of 50-lb \textbf{blocks of cheese} selected.
      \item The \textbf{raters} are students from a large, introductory food-science class.
      \item Student \textbf{background} (urban vs rural) identified; 10 students from each background randomly selected.
      \item Each rater will taste 8 \textbf{bites} of cheese in random order; there are 2 ``bites" of each of the 4 different types (raters don't know this).
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}

\framebreak

What are the sources of variability?
\begin{itemize}
  \item Variability due to different cheese \alert{types}.
  \item Variability due to \alert{background}.
  \item Variability due to different \alert{raters}.
  \item Random error across bites.
\end{itemize}
  
\framebreak

\begin{itemize}
  \item The Raters are \alert{nested} in Backgrounds (i.e., rater 1 has only one of the backgrounds, rural vs urban).
  \item Samples (or bites) are \alert{nested} in Rater (they don't share bites).
  \item Each rater sees each cheese type, so they are \alert{crossed}.
  \item Similarly, each background sees each cheese type, so they are also \alert{crossed}.
  \item The random error across bites is nested in the Rater-by-Type interaction (and Rater itself is nested in Background).
\end{itemize}
  
  \framebreak

\begin{itemize}
  \item Finally, the model. The factors are Cheese type (C), Background (B), and Rater (R). The Bites serve as our measurement units (error). An appropriate model would be:
  $$
  y_{ijkl} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_{j(i)} + \gamma_k + (\alpha\gamma)_{ik} + (\beta\gamma)_{jk(i)} + \epsilon_{l(ijk)},
  $$
  \item $\alpha_i$ (background) and $\gamma_k$ (cheese type) are fixed effects, and are fully crossed.
  \item $\beta_{j(i)}$ is the rater (random) effect, and is nested in background. It is crossed with cheese type, giving an interaction (random) effect: $(\beta\gamma)_{jk(i)}$.
\end{itemize}
  
\framebreak

<<cheese-example, eval=FALSE>>=
# Assuming 'cheese' data is loaded
# Type (C) and Background (B) are fixed and crossed.
# Rater (R) is random, nested in Background (B).

mod_cheese <- lmer(score ~ Type * Background + 
                     (1 | Background:Rater) + 
                     (1 | Background:Rater:Type), 
                   data = cheese)

# (1 | Background:Rater) accounts for the random variance of the raters.
# (1 | Background:Rater:Type) accounts for the random interaction: 
#   how individual raters react differently to different cheese types.
#   The Background is included because of the nesting.
#   Because we have 2 bites per combination, this can be separated from residual error.
@

\mode<article>{
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Our mathematical model has Rater-by-type interaction, not Rater-by-type-by-background. Why then does the R code show \texttt{(1 | Background:Rater:Type)} rather than \texttt{(1 | Rater:Type)}? 
    \item This is that same data-encoding trap that we had previously: if each rater is given a unique identifier (e.g., Rater is listed 1--20, rather than 1--10, with repeated values based on background), then we actually \emph{could} use \texttt{(1 | Rater:Type)}. In that case, the full model would be:
    \begin{center}
      \texttt{score ~ Type * Background + 
                     (1 | Rater) + 
                     (1 | Rater:Type)}
    \end{center}
    We need to think carefully about how the data are encoded!
  \end{itemize}
}

\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Designs with Crossed and Nested Factors}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Designs can have both crossed an nested factors.
    \item In addition to a nesting structure, there can be treatment factors that can be combined and assigned at random to units such that all combinations of nesting factors and treatment factors get an equal number of units.
    \item \textbf{Figuring out which factors are crossed and which are nested, and the appropriate model, can be challenging.} The best way to figure it out is practice!
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Example: Gum arabic (Example 12.1)}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Gum arabic is used to lengthen the shelf life of food items.
    \item We wish to see how different gums and gum preparation effect shelf-life.
  \end{itemize}
  
  \begin{block}{Gum arabic design}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item We obtain 4 raw gum samples from each of 2 varieties of trees (8 total samples).
      \item Each sample is split into 2 subsamples (16 total).
      \item One of the subsamples (randomly selected) will be demineralized, the other will not.
      \item All 16 subsamples are dried, and we make 5 measurements from each subsample.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
  
  \framebreak
  
  First, what are the sources of variability?
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Variance from Tree variety (2-levels)
    \item Variance from random samples from each tree (4-levels for each variety)
    \item Variance from the treatment effects (2 levels)
    \item Variance of the sub-sample*
    \mode<article>{ *(The interaction of sample and treatment effect defines the sub-sample; this is already accounted for in any interaction effects of sample and treatment, as we'll see later).}
  \end{itemize}
  
  \pagebreak
  
  \begin{itemize}
    \item The larger samples are \alert{nested} in tree variety;
    \item The subsamples are \alert{nested} in the samples.
    \item Subsamples get applied one of two treatments (demineralization)
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Because each subsample gets exactly one of each treatment, each sample gets both levels of the treatment, and sample and treatment are \alert{crossed}.
      \item Similarly, each variety of tree gets both levels of treatment, so variety and treatment are \alert{crossed}.
    \end{itemize}
    \item The five individual measurements within a single subsample are nested in subsample, or the variety-sample-treatment combinations.
  \end{itemize}

\framebreak

\begin{itemize}
  \item Finally, the model. Let Variety, Sample, and demineralization (treatment) be factors $A$, $B$, and $C$. An appropriate model would be:
  $$
  y_{ijkl} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_{j(i)} + \gamma_k + (\alpha\gamma)_{ik} + (\beta\gamma)_{jk(i)} + \epsilon_{l(ijk)},
  $$
  \item $\alpha_i$ and $\gamma_k$ (and their interaction) are fixed effects, and are fully crossed.
  \item $\beta_{j(i)}$ is the sample (random) effect, and is nested in variety. It is crossed with treatment, giving an interaction (random) effect: $(\beta\gamma)_{jk(i)}$.
\end{itemize}
  
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks,fragile]{Gum Example Code in R}

  %  \begin{itemize}
  %   \item Translating the Gum Arabic design into \texttt{lme4} syntax requires specifying the fixed crossed terms and the random nested terms separately.
  % \end{itemize}

<<gum-example, eval=FALSE>>=
# Assuming 'gum' data is loaded
# Variety (A) and Demin (C) are fixed and crossed.
# Sample (B) is random, nested in Variety.

mod_gum <- lmer(ShelfLife ~ Variety * Demin + 
                  (1 | Variety:Sample) + 
                  (1 | Variety:Sample:Demin), 
                data = gum)

# (1 | Variety:Sample) accounts for the random variance of the samples.
# (1 | Variety:Sample:Demin) accounts for the random interaction: 
# how individual samples react differently to demineralization.
@

\end{frame}

\section{Building models}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Recommendations for building models}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Building designs and models for nested, mixed-effects models is not easy, and takes some practice. Some recommended things to keep in mind:
    \item \textbf{Identify sources of variability}. These are things that we think might be important to account for, both intentional treatments and also natural structure of the data. These become the \alert{Factors} in our model.
    \item \textbf{Determine Nested vs Crossed}. Identify which (if any) of the factors are nested, and which are crossed.
    \item \textbf{Identify fixed vs Random Effects}. Are the specific sources of variance of interest, or are they more of a nuisance / sample of larger population?
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{Hasse Diagrams}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Visualizing Design: Hasse Diagrams}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item As designs get complex, keeping track of what is nested inside what, and what crosses with what, becomes incredibly difficult.
    \item \textbf{Hasse Diagrams} \citep{Lohr95} are graphical representations of a model showing the exact nesting/crossing and random/fixed structure.
    \item While historically used to manually derive F tests and random effects estimates, they are useful for building \texttt{lmer} formulas and understanding designs.
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[allowframebreaks]{Rules for Drawing a Hasse Diagram}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Start at the top with a node \textbf{M} for the Grand Mean.
    \item Add nodes below M for each factor that is not nested. Draw lines connecting them to M.
    \item \textbf{Random factors go in parentheses:} e.g., (Jar)
    \item For nested factors, draw them \textit{below} the factor they nest in, connected by a line (e.g., Brand $\rightarrow$ (Jar)).
    \item For crossed interactions, draw a node below the interacting main effects, with lines connecting up to both parents.
    \item \textbf{Infection Rule:} Any term located below a random term in the diagram becomes random itself, and must be in parentheses!
    \item End at the bottom with a node for \textbf{(E)} Error.
  \end{enumerate}
  
  \mode<article>{
  Drawing these on the board for the Carrots and Gum Arabic examples is highly recommended here! You can show how (Sample) drops cleanly below Variety, but Demineralization sits at the top level and crosses down into the nested structure.
  }
\end{frame}

